\title{Fijian, Western Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Western Fijian (ISO 639-3: wyy) is an Oceanic language of Fiji, distinct from Eastern (Bauan) Fijian, the standard. It covers several dialects, including Nadroga, Ba and Wayan. Published resources are mostly for the Wayan dialect, and I found no school curriculum materials.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes specific to Western Fijian were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Pawley and Sayaba, \emph{Words of Waya: A dictionary of the Wayan dialect of the Western Fijian language} (ANU, open access): \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/items/45052c6d-54d9-4bd4-8d53-b5a1751dcb87}.
\item Triffitt, ``The dialects of the Yasawa Islands of Fiji'' (Pacific Linguistics, 2000), on variation in phonology, grammar and lexicon: \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/07f5c73a-ff97-4b28-9a63-aba3fc38fc2a/download}.
\item Pawley's \emph{A Short Grammar of Wayan} (2018) is listed in Grambank: \url{https://grambank.clld.org/languages/waya1268}. I did not confirm its publisher.
\item Overview: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Fijian_language}.
\item Nadroga dialect article (short, flagged as needing citations): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nadroga_dialect}.
\item Omniglot overview: \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/westernfijian.htm}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Archival Nadroga materials, namely Arthur Capell papers with vocabulary lists in the Paradisec collection: \url{https://paradisec.org.au/fieldnotes/09.htm}.
\item SOAS library manuscript ``Dialects of Bau, Nadroga and Ba of Fiji'': \url{https://library.soas.ac.uk/Record/PP_MS_3.03.04}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Western Fijian is spoken by tens of thousands of people, but the published materials are linguistic and cover mainly Waya and the Yasawa dialects. I found no Ba or Nadi dictionary or grammar. A curriculum would need to choose a dialect, probably Nadroga or Wayan, and write teaching materials from these sources. Fijian schooling generally uses the standard Eastern variety.

\end{document}
